</a>
<header class="entry-header">
</header>
<div class="entry-content">
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lsr1.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: vietnam.vn</div>
</div>
<h3>Introductory Article:</h3>
<h4>• General Introduction</h4>
<p>
    The art of the Unicorn, Lion, and Dragon Dance is a distinctive form of folk performance among the Chinese community in Ho Chi Minh City, reflecting their rich spiritual and cultural life. <br/>
In traditional beliefs and culture, the three mythical creatures — the Unicorn, Lion, and Dragon — symbolize good fortune, prosperity, and the dispelling of bad luck. They often appear in major celebrations such as Lunar New Year, Mid-Autumn Festival, grand openings, and groundbreaking ceremonies. <br/>
Today, the Unicorn–Lion–Dragon dance has evolved and spread widely across the city and the country, becoming a familiar and cherished cultural performance for many Vietnamese people.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lsr2.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: cacchuongtrinhmuctieuquocgia.baodantoc.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Origins</h4>
<p>
    The art of the Unicorn–Lion–Dragon dance was first brought to the Saigon–Chợ Lớn area by early Chinese settlers more than a century ago. Throughout the history of this land’s formation and development, the art has continuously evolved, been passed down, and spread widely across the city and to other provinces such as Đồng Nai and Vũng Tàu. <br/>
Today, this cultural heritage is still widely practiced within the community, especially during festivals, grand openings, and groundbreaking ceremonies. In Ho Chi Minh City and many other localities, numerous guilds and Unicorn–Lion–Dragon troupes continue to grow and perform regularly each year, serving the people’s cultural and entertainment needs — particularly during the Lunar New Year season. Many troupes have even expanded internationally, performing in countries such as France, Norway, the United States, and Germany. <br/>
In 2022, the Unicorn–Lion–Dragon dance was officially included as a competitive event in Vietnam’s 9th National Sports Festival. In April 2023, the Vietnam Unicorn–Lion–Dragon Federation was established, further honoring and elevating this traditional folk art to new heights. The current competition rules are largely based on international standards. By 2025, this art form was officially recognized by the state as a National Intangible Cultural Heritage.
  </p><div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lsr3.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: vtv.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Performance Art</h4>
<p>
    The Unicorn–Lion–Dragon dance is a harmonious blend of music, choreography, and martial arts performance. Each dance can feature a single mythical creature or a combination of all three, depending on the purpose of the event. Performances may pair the lion with the unicorn, the unicorn with the dragon, or all three together. The movements in the Unicorn–Lion–Dragon dance carry strong influences from traditional Vietnamese martial arts. This art form requires performers to possess great physical strength, flexibility, and refined technical skills. <br/>
A key figure in these performances is Ông Địa (the Earth God) — a cheerful, big-bellied man wearing a smiling mask, holding a paper fan, and playfully leading the unicorn to entertain the crowd and bring joy to the hosts. The close companionship between Ông Địa and the unicorn symbolizes the harmony between all living beings in a peaceful and prosperous world. Ông Địa is believed to be the incarnation of Maitreya Buddha, who tamed wild creatures and brought blessings to humankind. <br/>
Thần Tài (the God of Wealth) is the deity of fortune and prosperity, believed to bring luck and good business. That is why shop owners and businesses often invite the Unicorn–Lion–Dragon troupe to perform at openings or festive occasions. Typically, the trio of Unicorn, Ông Địa, and Thần Tài appear together in performances to express wishes for good luck, success, and abundance.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lsr4.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: backstage.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Cultural Beauty</h4>
<p>
    According to Eastern beliefs, the unicorn, lion, and dragon are three sacred creatures symbolizing prosperity, wealth, and happiness. <br/>
Thus, during festivals such as Lunar New Year or the Mid-Autumn Festival, the lively sights and sounds of Unicorn–Lion–Dragon troupes — accompanied by Ông Địa (the Earth God) — can be found throughout streets and neighborhoods. Their energetic and spiritual performances bring joy and excitement to communities, especially to children. During the Lunar New Year, many families still eagerly prepare red envelopes of “lucky money” to greet the troupes, hoping for a prosperous and smooth beginning to the year. <br/>
Today, Unicorn–Lion–Dragon troupes across the country have creatively combined the distinctive features of this martial–art-based performance with traditional Vietnamese cultural elements, shaping a strong Vietnamese identity within this art form, proudly created and developed by local artists. <br/>
For a performance to be vivid and successful, the rhythm of drums, gongs, and cymbals plays a vital role. Each performer must undergo disciplined training to harmonize movements with the beat — from greeting, bowing, lying down, eating, climbing up, to descending — while expressing various emotions such as joy, sadness, excitement, curiosity, and anger. <br/>
The soul of the dance lies within the tempo of the drums — sometimes slow and graceful, other times fast and intense like battle drums — capturing the majesty and strength of the unicorn, lion, and dragon, and creating a vibrant, captivating atmosphere that deeply impresses audiences.
  </p>
</div>
